# Kriegsspiel Table License

Kriegsspiel Table is licensed under the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0, set out in full below, together with
the terms in this section. This section is part of the license, and where it and the PolyForm terms differ, this
section applies. Section 2 applies over section 1: a use listed in section 2 needs a commercial license even where
it also fits section 1. Section 3 applies over everything else, sections 1 and 2 included.

These terms cover Kriegsspiel Table: the app, its converter, the packs that come with it, its source code, and any
changed version of any of them or work based on them ("the software"). The licensor is Bruno Firme.

## 1. Allowed without a commercial license

Whether or not money is involved, and whatever the PolyForm terms below say of commercial purposes, anyone may:

- play, umpire, host and join games with the software, and teach, study and research with it;
- run events with it, free or paid: club nights, conventions, tournaments and courses, at home, at a club or at a
  shop, café or other venue, charging entry, fees or membership dues;
- stream, record and publish games played on it, including for income from adverts, sponsors or supporters;
- change the software and share it, changed or not, free of charge, with these terms and the Required Notice line
  above;
- make packs, pieces, scenarios, maps, rules, pictures and scripts with it, and share them free of charge.

A person working on their own, such as a freelance umpire or a tutor running a paid course, is covered by this
section, except when working for a company or firm as described in 2(c), or for anyone described in section 3.

## 2. Needs a commercial license

A commercial license from the licensor is needed to:

(a) sell the software or anything that contains it, or charge for access to it, including as an online or hosted
    service. Hosting a game for others with the app's Host a game button is playing, under section 1, not this;
(b) sell packs, pieces or scenarios made for the software: anything in its file formats, or made with its piece
    studio or its converter, alone or with other things;
(c) use the software in the work of a company or firm run for profit: running wargames, exercises or simulations
    for clients, training or planning with its own staff, or publishing or shipping it in a product. Covered by
    section 1 instead are a shop, venue or event organiser running games for players; a school, university or other
    educational institution teaching with it, whoever runs it; a charity or club, however it is incorporated; and a
    company's staff playing in their own time;
(d) use it in any way described in section 3.

To sell means to offer in exchange for money or anything else of value, including behind a paywall, a paid
subscription or a paid tier. Voluntary tips and donations are not selling, as long as nothing is held back from
those who do not give.

Work of your own that does not need the software to be used, such as a rulebook, a map drawn in another program, or
a picture, remains yours to sell anywhere; these terms do not reach it. What 2(b) needs a license for is selling
packs, pieces and scenarios for the software. These terms give no rights in other people's work brought into the
software, such as a converted module or someone else's map: that stays under its owners' terms.

## 3. Defence and national security

Use by or for any of the following, in any country, needs a commercial license, whatever section 1 and the PolyForm
terms below say, including what they say of government institutions, educational institutions and public research
organisations:

- armed forces, including their reserves, academies, staff colleges and war colleges;
- ministries, departments and agencies of defence;
- intelligence and national-security agencies;
- anyone acting for any of them or paid by any of them for the work, such as contractors, consultancies, think
  tanks and research groups, including research they commission or pay for.

People who belong to any of these and play, umpire or study in their own time, not as part of their service or
work, are hobbyists like anyone else and covered by section 1.

Other government use is allowed as the PolyForm terms below say.

## 4. Commercial licenses

To ask for a commercial license, contact Bruno Firme at firma.eht@outlook.com (or on Discord: b.firme). Terms are
agreed case by case.

